# CoW Track 1A — list comprehension over `&coll` reads correctly (yields-empty fix).
#
# `[x * 2 for x in &a]` over a populated vector wrongly yielded an EMPTY vector on
# Rust gg: the comprehension lowered `&a` to a Ptr but never auto-deref'd it, so
# the length read hit the pointer's own bytes → 0 iterations. The shared
# iterable-deref (the same one the statement-for loop uses) fixes the READ so the
# comprehension doubles every element. Comprehension element WRITE-THROUGH is
# deferred — this pins the read path only.
# Output must be correct on BOTH backends.

void main():
    Vector[int] a = [1, 2, 3]
    Vector[int] b = [x * 2 for x in &a]
    print(b.len())
    print(b.get(0).unwrap())
    print(b.get(1).unwrap())
    print(b.get(2).unwrap())
